deep.reconstruction:
Setting, from the previous pages. X is an algebraic variety over a field K (think of a number field, with X(C) carrying the transcendental topology). X_K is the set of K-rational points. Γ = Gal(K̄/K), and Π_1(X) is the (profinitely completed) fundamental groupoid of X(C).

For a path class l: x → y with x, y in X_K and u in Γ, Grothendieck has defined two elements measuring how far l is from being Galois-invariant:
- f_l(u) in π̂_1(X,x);
- g_l(u) in π̂_1(X,y), essentially u(l)·l^{-1}.
These satisfy:
- the passage formulas (9) relating f and g;
- the composition rule (10)/(11), g_{l'l}(u) = g_{l'}(u) · l'(g_l(u));
- its iterate (12).
So for fixed l, g_l is a 1-cocycle of Γ with values in π̂_1(X,y). The corresponding torsor is Hom_{Π_1}(x,y).

This page introduces a device that simplifies all of these formulas. Take a 1-connected (path-connected, simply connected) space T with a continuous map p: T → X, and set T_K = p^{-1}(X_K).
- Since T is simply connected, any two points x, y of T are joined by a unique homotopy class of paths. Its image is a canonical arrow l_{y,x}: p(x) → p(y) in Π_1(X) (16). This arrow depends on the lifts x, y, not only on p(x), p(y); p need not be injective.
- Hence the groups π_1(X,p(x)), for x in T, form a transitive system of isomorphisms. Its 'limit' π = π_1(X;T) (14) is the fundamental group 'based at T'. It comes with canonical isomorphisms φ_x: π → π_1(X,p(x)) (15).
- For x, y in T_K, transport through φ_y (for g) and φ_x (for f) turns f_{l_{y,x}}(u) and g_{l_{y,x}}(u) into one and the same element g^{T/X}_{y,x}(u) of π (17).

On the next page this yields:
- the cocycle/Chasles relation g_{zy}(u) g_{yx}(u) = g_{zx}(u) (18) and its iterate (19);
- the passage formulas (9) disappear;
- ordinary paths are recovered by taking T = [0,1];
- functoriality along maps T → T' over X (21);
- a marginal hint at 'infinitesimal paths' at a rational point, i.e. what are now called tangential base points.

Purpose: a clean bookkeeping formalism for the Galois action on profinite fundamental groupoids with many rational base points, in the Teichmüller-tower setting.
